\label{ch:dependent-type-sys}

Dependent type systems erase the boundary between types and terms: types can depend on values, and values can inhabit types that encode logical propositions. This unification, rooted in the Curry--Howard correspondence, gives rise to programming languages that are simultaneously proof assistants and general-purpose programming languages. This chapter develops the theory of dependent types from Martin-L\"of's type theory through modern implementations in Agda, Coq, Lean, and Idris.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 47.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Dependent Function Types (Pi Types)}\label{sec:pi-types}

The dependent function type, or \emph{Pi type}, generalizes the ordinary function type $\tau_1 \to \tau_2$ by allowing the return type to depend on the value of the argument.

\subsection{Syntax and Typing}\label{sec:pi-syntax}

\begin{grammar}{Dependent Function Types}
  \production{A, B}{\ldots \mid \Pi x : A.\, B} \\
  \production{M, N}{\ldots \mid \lambda x.\, M \mid M\; N}
\end{grammar}

The type $\Pi x : A.\, B$ is the type of functions that take an argument $x$ of type $A$ and return a result of type $B$, where $B$ may mention $x$. When $B$ does not mention $x$, we recover the ordinary function type: $A \to B \equiv \Pi x : A.\, B$.

\begin{typerules}{Pi Types}
  \typerule{Pi-Form}{A : \mathcal{U}_i \quad x : A \vdash B : \mathcal{U}_j}
            {\Pi x : A.\, B : \mathcal{U}_{\max(i,j)}} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs}{x : A \vdash M : B}
            {\lambda x.\, M : \Pi x : A.\, B} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{App}{f : \Pi x : A.\, B \quad a : A}
            {f\; a : B[a/x]}
\end{typerules}

\subsection{Examples}\label{sec:pi-examples}

\begin{example}[Vector type]\label{ex:vector}
The type of vectors of length $n$ with elements of type $A$:
\[
  \mathrm{Vec} : \mathrm{Nat} \to \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{U}
\]
The function $\mathtt{head} : \Pi n : \mathrm{Nat}.\, \Pi A : \mathcal{U}.\, \mathrm{Vec}\; (n + 1)\; A \to A$ takes a non-empty vector and returns its first element. The return type $A$ does not depend on $n$, but the argument type $\mathrm{Vec}\; (n + 1)\; A$ does.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Finite types]\label{ex:finite}
The type $\mathrm{Fin}\; n$ of natural numbers less than $n$:
\[
  \mathrm{Fin} : \mathrm{Nat} \to \mathcal{U}
\]
$\mathrm{Fin}\; 0$ is empty; $\mathrm{Fin}\; (n + 1)$ has constructors $\mathtt{zero} : \mathrm{Fin}\; (n + 1)$ and $\mathtt{suc} : \mathrm{Fin}\; n \to \mathrm{Fin}\; (n + 1)$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Semantics}\label{sec:pi-semantics}

The reduction rule for Pi types is beta-reduction:
\[
  (\lambda x.\, M)\; N \longrightarrow M[N/x]
\]
Two Pi types $\Pi x : A.\, B$ and $\Pi x : A'.\, B'$ are definitionally equal if $A \equiv A'$ and $B \equiv B'$ (under $\alpha$-conversion).

