\section{Dependent Pair Types (Sigma Types)}\label{sec:sigma-types}

The dependent pair type, or \emph{Sigma type}, generalizes the product type $A \times B$ by allowing the type of the second component to depend on the value of the first.

\subsection{Syntax and Typing}\label{sec:sigma-syntax}

\begin{grammar}{Dependent Pair Types}
  \production{A, B}{\ldots \mid \Sigma x : A.\, B} \\
  \production{M, N}{\ldots \mid (M, N) \mid \mathtt{let}\; (x, y) = M\; \mathtt{in}\; N}
\end{grammar}

\begin{typerules}{Sigma Types}
  \typerule{Sigma-Form}{A : \mathcal{U}_i \quad x : A \vdash B : \mathcal{U}_j}
            {\Sigma x : A.\, B : \mathcal{U}_{\max(i,j)}} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Pair}{a : A \quad b : B[a/x]}
            {(a, b) : \Sigma x : A.\, B} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Fst}{p : \Sigma x : A.\, B}
            {\mathtt{fst}\; p : A} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Snd}{p : \Sigma x : A.\, B}
            {\mathtt{snd}\; p : B[\mathtt{fst}\; p / x]}
\end{typerules}

\subsection{Relationship to Existential Types}\label{sec:sigma-existential}

Sigma types are the constructive counterpart of existential types. Under the Curry--Howard correspondence, $\Sigma x : A.\, B$ corresponds to the logical statement ``there exists an $x$ of type $A$ such that $B(x)$ holds.'' A proof of this statement is a pair $(a, b)$ where $a$ is a witness and $b$ is a proof that $B(a)$ holds.

\begin{example}[Existential package]\label{ex:existential}
An existential package for an abstract type $T$ with an operation $\mathtt{op} : T \to T$:
\[
  \Sigma T : \mathcal{U}.\, (T \to T) \times (T \to \mathrm{Bool})
\]
A value of this type is a triple $(T, \mathtt{op}, \mathtt{test})$ where $T$ is a concrete type, $\mathtt{op}$ is an operation on $T$, and $\mathtt{test}$ is a predicate on $T$.
\end{example}

