\section{Inductive Families}\label{sec:inductive-families}

Inductive families generalize inductive types by allowing the type of each constructor to specify indices that constrain the result type.

\subsection{Definition}\label{sec:inductive-families-def}

\begin{definition}[Inductive Family]\label{def:inductive-family}
An \emph{inductive family} is a family of types $A : I \to \mathcal{U}$ defined by a set of constructors, each of which has a type of the form:
\[
  c : \Pi x_1 : B_1.\, \ldots \Pi x_n : B_n.\, A\; i
\]
where $i$ is an index expression that may depend on $x_1, \ldots, x_n$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Example: Vectors}\label{sec:vectors}

\begin{verbatim}
  data Vec : Nat -> Type -> Type where
    nil  : Vec zero A
    cons : A -> Vec n A -> Vec (suc n) A
\end{verbatim}

The type $\mathrm{Vec}\; n\; A$ is indexed by the length $n$ and the element type $A$. The constructor $\mathtt{nil}$ produces a vector of length zero; the constructor $\mathtt{cons}$ produces a vector of length $n + 1$ from an element and a vector of length $n$.

\subsection{Example: Equality}\label{sec:equality-type}

The propositional equality type is an inductive family:

\begin{verbatim}
  data _==_ {A : Type} : A -> A -> Type where
    refl : {x : A} -> x == x
\end{verbatim}

The type $x == y$ is inhabited only when $x$ and $y$ are definitionally equal. The constructor $\mathtt{refl}$ proves $x == x$ for any $x$.

\subsection{Elimination}\label{sec:inductive-elim}

Inductive families support \emph{dependent elimination}: a function defined by pattern matching on an inductive family can refine its type based on the constructor matched.

\begin{example}[Vector append]\label{ex:vec-append}
\begin{verbatim}
  append : {n m : Nat} -> {A : Type} -> Vec n A -> Vec m A -> Vec (n + m) A
  append nil       ys = ys
  append (cons x xs) ys = cons x (append xs ys)
\end{verbatim}
The type of $\mathtt{append}$ states that appending a vector of length $n$ and a vector of length $m$ produces a vector of length $n + m$. The pattern matching refines the type: in the first clause, $n = 0$ and the result is $\mathrm{Vec}\; m\; A$; in the second clause, $n = k + 1$ and the result is $\mathrm{Vec}\; (k + 1 + m)\; A$.
\end{example}

