\section{Universe Hierarchies}\label{sec:universe-hierarchies}

To avoid paradoxes (notably Girard's paradox, which is the type-theoretic counterpart of Russell's paradox), dependent type systems stratify types into a hierarchy of \emph{universes}.

\subsection{The Problem}\label{sec:universe-problem}

If we allow $\mathcal{U} : \mathcal{U}$ (a universe that contains itself), we can encode Girard's paradox, which yields a term of the empty type. This means the system is inconsistent: every type is inhabited, and the logic is trivial.

\subsection{Cumulative Universes}\label{sec:cumulative}

The standard solution is a hierarchy of universes:
\[
  \mathcal{U}_0 : \mathcal{U}_1 : \mathcal{U}_2 : \cdots
\]
where $\mathcal{U}_i : \mathcal{U}_{i+1}$. The hierarchy is \emph{cumulative}: if $A : \mathcal{U}_i$, then $A : \mathcal{U}_{i+1}$ (and $A : \mathcal{U}_j$ for all $j > i$).

\begin{typerules}{Universe Rules}
  \typeaxiom{Univ}{\mathcal{U}_i : \mathcal{U}_{i+1}} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Cumul}{A : \mathcal{U}_i}
            {A : \mathcal{U}_{i+1}}
\end{typerules}

\subsection{Universe Polymorphism}\label{sec:universe-poly}

Universe polymorphism allows definitions to work at any universe level:

\begin{verbatim}
  id : {i : Level} -> {A : U i} -> A -> A
  id {i} {A} x = x
\end{verbatim}

Here, $\mathtt{id}$ is polymorphic over the universe level $i$. It can be applied to types in $\mathcal{U}_0$, $\mathcal{U}_1$, or any other level.

\subsection{Implementation in Proof Assistants}\label{sec:universe-impl}

