\section{Equality and Equality Elimination}\label{sec:equality}

Propositional equality in dependent type theory is a rich structure. The equality type $a =_A b$ is itself an inductive family, and its elimination principle (the \emph{J rule}) is the foundation of all equality reasoning.

\subsection{The J Rule}\label{sec:j-rule}

\begin{typerules}{Equality Elimination (J Rule)}
  \typerule{J}{x : A, y : A, p : x = y \vdash P(x, y, p) \;\mathrm{type} \quad
                x : A \vdash d : P(x, x, \mathtt{refl})}
            {x : A, y : A, p : x = y \vdash J(x, y, p, d) : P(x, y, p)}
\end{typerules}

The J rule states that to prove a property $P$ for all equalities $p : x = y$, it suffices to prove it for reflexivity ($x = x$). This is the \emph{indiscernibility of identicals}: if $x = y$, then any property of $x$ is a property of $y$.

\subsection{Derived Principles}\label{sec:equality-derived}

From the J rule, we can derive:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Symmetry:} $p : x = y \implies p^{-1} : y = x$
  \item \textbf{Transitivity:} $p : x = y, q : y = z \implies p \cdot q : x = z$
  \item \textbf{Congruence:} $p : x = y \implies f(p) : f(x) = f(y)$
  \item \textbf{Transport (subst):} $p : x = y, v : P(x) \implies \mathtt{transport}^P(p, v) : P(y)$
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Univalence}\label{sec:univalence}

The \emph{univalence axiom} (Voevodsky~\cite{voevodsky2010}) states that equivalent types are equal:
\[
  (A \simeq B) \simeq (A =_{\mathcal{U}} B)
\]
This means that isomorphic types can be identified, enabling a form of structural reasoning about types. Univalence is consistent with Martin-L\"of type theory (as shown by the simplicial model~\cite{kapulkin2012}) and is adopted as an axiom in the Cubical Agda and as a computed feature in Cubical Type Theory~\cite{cohen2017}.

