\section{Implementations: Agda, Coq, Lean, Idris}\label{sec:dependent-implementations}

\subsection{Agda}\label{sec:agda}

Agda is a dependently-typed functional programming language based on Martin-L\"of type theory. Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Dependent pattern matching.} Functions are defined by pattern matching, with the type checker verifying exhaustiveness and consistency.
  \item \textbf{Universe polymorphism.} Explicit universe levels allow definitions to work at any level.
  \item \textbf{Cubical mode.} Agda's cubical mode implements Cubical Type Theory, providing computational univalence and higher inductive types.
  \item \textbf{Termination checking.} Agda checks that all recursive functions terminate, ensuring consistency.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Coq}\label{sec:coq}

Coq is based on the \emph{Calculus of Inductive Constructions} (CIC), which combines the Calculus of Constructions (CoC) with inductive types. Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Inductive types.} A rich system of inductive types with dependent elimination.
  \item \textbf{Propositional truncation.} The \texttt{Prop} universe is proof-irrelevant: all proofs of a proposition are considered equal.
  \item \textbf{Extraction.} Coq programs can be extracted to OCaml, Haskell, or Scheme for execution.
  \item \textbf{Tactic-based proving.} Coq's tactic language allows interactive construction of proofs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Lean}\label{sec:lean}

Lean is a theorem prover and programming language developed by de Moura~\cite{demoura2015}. Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Elaboration.} Lean's elaborator performs extensive type inference, including universe inference and implicit argument insertion.
  \item \textbf{Tactic framework.} Lean 4 provides a metaprogramming framework for writing custom tactics in Lean itself.
  \item \textbf{Mathlib.} Lean's mathematical library (Mathlib) is one of the largest formalized mathematics libraries.
  \item \textbf{Quotient types.} Lean supports quotient types as a primitive, enabling reasoning about equivalence classes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Idris}\label{sec:idris}

Idris is a general-purpose dependently-typed programming language. Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Erasability analysis.} Idris determines which arguments can be erased at compile time, enabling efficient compilation.
  \item \textbf{Effects system.} Idris 1 had a built-in effects system for tracking side effects in types.
  \item \textbf{Resource-dependent types.} Idris 2 supports linear types for resource management.
  \item \textbf{Code generation.} Idris compiles to C, JavaScript, and other backends.
\end{itemize}

