\begin{historical}
Dependent type theory originates in Per Martin-L\"of's work on the foundations of mathematics in the 1970s~\cite{martinlof1975}. His intuitionistic type theory was designed to provide a foundation for constructive mathematics, where proofs are programs and propositions are types. The Curry--Howard correspondence, which identifies propositions with types and proofs with programs, gives dependent types their dual role as both a programming language feature and a logical system. The development of the Calculus of Constructions by Coquand and Huet~\cite{coquand1988} and its extension with inductive types (CIC) by Paulin-Mohring~\cite{paulin1993} provided the theoretical foundation for Coq. The modern era of dependent types, with implementations like Agda, Lean, and Idris, has made these ideas accessible to working programmers and mathematicians.
\end{historical}

