\subsection{Large Elimination}\label{sec:large-elimination}

\emph{Large elimination} allows a function defined by pattern matching on an inductive type to return a \emph{type} rather than a \emph{value}. This is essential for defining type-level functions by recursion.

\begin{example}[Vector to list conversion]\label{ex:vec-to-list}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Large elimination: match on length to produce a type
  vecToList : {n : Nat} -> Vec n A -> List A
  vecToList nil = []
  vecToList (cons x xs) = x :: vecToList xs
  -- The return type List A does not depend on n,
  -- but the argument type Vec n A does.
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Type-level function by recursion]\label{ex:type-level-rec}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Tuple type of a given arity:
  NTuple : Nat -> Type -> Type
  NTuple zero A = Unit
  NTuple (suc n) A = A * NTuple n A
  -- NTuple 3 Int = Int * Int * Int * Unit
\end{verbatim}
This is large elimination because $\mathrm{NTuple}$ returns a type ($\mathcal{U}$), not a value.
\end{example}

\subsection{Pattern Matching and Coverage Checking}\label{sec:coverage-checking}

Dependent pattern matching requires the type checker to verify that all cases are covered and that the right-hand sides are well-typed.

\begin{definition}[Coverage Checking]\label{def:coverage}
A pattern match is \emph{exhaustive} if every possible value of the matched type is covered by at least one clause. The coverage checker constructs a \emph{splitting tree} that decomposes the type into constructors and verifies that every leaf is covered.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Coverage checking for Fin]\label{ex:fin-coverage}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Fin (suc n) has two constructors: zero and suc
  -- Both must be covered:
  elim : {n : Nat} -> Fin (suc n) -> Nat
  elim zero = 0
  elim (suc k) = elim k + 1
  -- Coverage: zero covers the zero case; suc covers the suc case.
  -- Both constructors of Fin (suc n) are covered.
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

