\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-47}

Dependent type systems represent the culmination of the Curry--Howard correspondence: types that encode propositions, programs that encode proofs, and computation that encodes proof simplification. The Pi and Sigma types generalize function and product types, inductive families generalize algebraic data types, and universe hierarchies ensure consistency.

The practical implementations---Agda, Coq, Lean, and Idris---demonstrate that dependent types can be used not only for formal verification but also for everyday programming. The challenges of type checking (undecidability of type conversion in general, the need for termination checking, the complexity of universe management) are active areas of research.

For further reading, we recommend Martin-L\"of's original lectures~\cite{martinlof1984}, the textbook by Bove, Dybjer, and Maler~\cite{bove2009}, and the Homotopy Type Theory book~\cite{hottbook}.
